Subject: A layout break moves the compiled code's cache epoch

What this does
  - `js_aot_layout_break` replaces the eight writes of `aot_layout_broken`: it sets the layout's bit and, the first time that layout breaks,
    moves `aot_pic_epoch` and counts it in `aot_epoch_bumps`. A layout breaks at most once, so it moves the epoch at most once.
    The interpreter's `ic_watchpoint_epoch` does not move.
  - Kit builds only (`QJS_KIT_TEST_KNOBS`): `QJS_AOT_NOEPOCHBUMP` sets the bit without moving the epoch, to show what a compiled body that
    only compares the epoch does then.

Why we need it
  A compiled body already compares the epoch after every safepoint. If a layout break moves it, the body needs no separate test of the layout
  bits next to that comparison.

Functions touched
  `js_aot_layout_break` (new), `js_aot_layout_leave`, `js_aot_content_store_slow`, `js_aot_content_enter_slow`, `js_aot_content_last`,
  `qjs_aot_layout_claim`, `qjs_aot_layout_fn_created`, and the prototype-held check in the shape code.

No.

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -16388,12 +16388,31 @@
    shape being LEFT is tested: one load of a field already in cache. The bit is
    permanent, like a map deprecation, and a body that relied on the layout goes
    to its generic body from then on. */
+
+/* A layout's promises are broken for good. The cache epoch moves with the first break of each
+   layout (one-way: at most once per layout), so a compiled body that compares the epoch after a
+   safepoint also learns of the break. The interpreter's `ic_watchpoint_epoch` does not move: a
+   layout break says nothing about interpreter IC entries. */
+static void js_aot_layout_break(JSRuntime *rt, uint32_t layout_id)
+{
+    uint64_t bit = 1ULL << (layout_id - 1);
+    if (rt->aot_layout_broken & bit)
+        return;
+    rt->aot_layout_broken |= bit;
+#ifdef QJS_KIT_TEST_KNOBS
+    if (getenv("QJS_AOT_NOEPOCHBUMP"))
+        return;
+#endif
+    rt->aot_epoch_bumps++;
+    rt->aot_pic_epoch++;
+}
